{"ts":"2026-11-15T02:06:18.284Z","actor":"claude","stage":"factcheck","action":"factcheck.review","details":{"verdict":"pass","notes":"Sources: AP via NPR (14 Nov 2026) and Spaceflight Now. Launch 13 Nov 2026 22:47 UTC (Friday the 13th). The ship reached orbit, released 26 Starlinks, flew 14 laps about 265 km up; caught 22 h after launch at the tower it left from; the first-ever capture of a Starship flying back from space; two arms; three previous booster catches; booster engines stopped early, diverted to the Gulf and self-destructed. 124 m, the world's largest rocket; SpaceX says the catch demonstrates full reusability; Starship is an Artemis lunar lander. The photos are NOT of Flight 15: they show the Flight 5 booster catch (13 Oct 2024, CC BY 2.0), and the on-screen chips say so. SpaceX's own photos are CC BY-NC and were not used. The 'full stack' photo was excluded because its author forbids cropping."},"gitSha":"d11260e"}
